WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.410
STEPHEN GREENBLATT: Shakespeare was the creator of mass entertainments.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:10.070
He was interested in reaching a very large number of people in his world.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:16.160
And he thought professionally about how to reach those people where they lived,

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.590
as it were, in their deepest way.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.970
I've thought about Shakespeare for many, many years.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:29.090
And I remain amazed by his intuitive ideas as to where the power would lie.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:34.760
He thought in Merchant of Venice about the relationship

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.780
between those who are in the inside and those

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.630
who are on the outside, between those who are comfortably at the center

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:44.680
and those who are the pariahs, the hated and spat upon ones.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.150
So he looked, for example, to women who didn't obey the rules,

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.980
as in Taming of the Shrew.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.680
He looked to the relationship between whites and blacks

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.500
and to thinking about sex anxieties in Othello.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.350
He thought about what to do with very old people in families,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:04.610
as in King Lear.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.780
He had what seems to us at this distance 400 years later

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.290
to be an unerring instinct for thinking about what might still concern us

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.720
even though our concerns are quite different from those

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.590
that governed Shakespeare's time.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.040
And that's part of the secret of his work.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.080
He was a master of metamorphosis.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.730
He allowed his plays to go out into the world and change their meanings.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.070
And 400 years from now, though we can scarcely

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.220
imagine what the world will be like, I think

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:41.020
we can be confident that there will be people, many, many people, who

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:47.570
will still respond in their own ways to what Shakespeare left them.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:52.980
This is part of the mystery and part of the thrill of Shakespeare's work.

